# Architecture rules

This is a **Python 3.14 / Django 6 / DRF / Celery** project on the
Cookiecutter-Django layout. All code MUST follow the rules below.

## 1. Project layout

```
config/                        # Django project (settings, urls, asgi, wsgi, celery_app)
  settings/{base,local,production,test}.py
seopartnerhub/                 # Django apps live here
  <app>/
    api/         # DRF views, serializers, urls
    services/    # use cases, orchestration, transactions
    selectors/   # read-only queries (no writes)
    tasks/       # Celery tasks
    integrations/ # adapters for external APIs / webhooks / n8n
    models.py | models/
    migrations/
    tests/
tests/                         # cross-app tests
specs/                         # Spec Kit feature specs
.specify/                      # Spec Kit infrastructure
```

## 2. OOP — services are classes

- Business logic lives in **service classes** under
  `seopartnerhub/<app>/services/`. Views, serializers, signals, and
  model methods MUST NOT contain business logic.
- Views translate HTTP ↔ services. Models hold data + invariants only.
- **One public class per file.** Filename = class name in `snake_case`
  (`class OutreachSender` → `outreach_sender.py`).
- Prefer **composition** over inheritance. Inject collaborators through
  `__init__`, not via module-level singletons or class-level state.
- Data containers outside the ORM: `pydantic.BaseModel` with
  `model_config = ConfigDict(frozen=True)`, or
  `@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)`.

## 3. SOLID

- **S** — one class, one reason to change.
- **O** — extend via new strategy classes, do not edit existing ones.
- **L** — subclasses are drop-in replacements for their base.
- **I** — many small abstractions (`typing.Protocol`), never one fat one.
- **D** — depend on **abstractions** (`Protocol` / `ABC`), not on
  concrete classes. Inject every collaborator through `__init__`.

## 4. Layering (dependency direction)

```
api/  →  services/  →  selectors/ + models + integrations(protocol)
                                  ↑
                          tasks/  (Celery wrappers around services)
```

- `api/` MAY import `services/` and serializers; MUST NOT import
  `tasks/` or `integrations/` directly.
- `services/` MAY import `selectors/`, `models`, and integration
  **protocols** (not concrete adapters); concrete adapters are injected
  in `apps.py` / DI factory.
- `selectors/` are read-only — no `.save()`, `.create()`, `.delete()`.
- `tasks/` wrap services, add retry/backoff/idempotency; never contain
  business logic of their own.

## 5. Typing

- `mypy` MUST pass (config in `pyproject.toml` with
  `mypy_django_plugin` + `mypy_drf_plugin`).
- **Never use `typing.Any`.** Use `object` + `isinstance`, generics,
  `TypedDict`, `Protocol`, or proper unions.
- Put `from __future__ import annotations` at the top of every module.
- Public methods MUST have complete type hints on parameters and return.
- `# type: ignore` requires a `# TODO` and a reason.

## 6. Configuration — single source of truth

- All runtime parameters live in `config/settings/{base,local,production,test}.py`,
  read via `django-environ` from `./.envs/.local/*` and
  `./.envs/.production/*`.
- **No hardcoded env-dependent values** anywhere outside `config/`.
- Adding a new parameter:
  1. Add a typed read in the relevant `settings/*.py`.
  2. Add the matching `KEY=value` line to `./.envs/.local/.django` (and
     `.production/.django` if used in prod).
  3. Read the value via `from django.conf import settings` or inject
     through a service constructor. Never `os.environ` outside `config/`.

## 7. Async & background work

- HTTP-bound code uses Django views (sync by default; async views only
  when there is a measurable benefit).
- Anything longer than ~200 ms or touching a third-party API MUST be a
  **Celery task**. Tasks MUST be **idempotent** and declare retry
  policy: `autoretry_for=(Exception,)`, `retry_backoff=True`,
  `max_retries=N`.
- External integrations go through an adapter under
  `seopartnerhub/<app>/integrations/` that implements a protocol
  defined next to the service. The service depends on the protocol, not
  on `requests` / `httpx` directly.

## 8. Error handling

- Prefer returning `Result | None` over raising for **expected**
  business outcomes (e.g. "lead not found").
- When you must raise, use a **static** message and pass dynamic data
  separately (e.g. `logger.exception("static msg", extra={"id": x})`).
- API errors MUST flow through a single DRF exception handler that
  produces `{ "error": { "code": ..., "message": ... } }`.
- Always log the **full** exception (`logger.exception(...)` or
  `logger.error(..., exc_info=True)`). Never log only `str(error)` or
  `error.message`.
- Bare `except:` and `except Exception: pass` are forbidden.
